---
/**
 * /quoted/ — the work that carries its own price, in one place.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. Everything this practice sells that is quoted in writing
 * before it starts appeared, until this page, in exactly ONE
 * place: the capability block on the three comparison sheets. A visitor who
 * never opened a comparison document could not learn that a small online store
 * is buyable here at all. They are not refusals and they are not free — they are
 * real work with a real price, and they had no door.
 *
 * IT PUBLISHES NO FIGURE, and that is what keeps D94 intact. That rule says
 * /services/ stops at five figures and any further product publishes on its own
 * page, because a price list becomes a menu once the prices stack. Nothing here
 * carries a number, so this page is a door rather than a sixth panel on a price
 * list.
 *
 * THE SHARED ITEMS ONLY, AND THE OMISSION IS DELIBERATE. The store
 * configurator lives in EXTRA under `stores` in src/lib/capabilities.js, which
 * is sheet-specific by design, and scripts/lib/capability-check.mjs asserts in
 * BOTH directions that a sheet's own items are ABSENT from its sisters. Pulling
 * the stores-only item onto a general page would not break that check — it does
 * not read this page — but it would break what the check MEANS, which is that
 * sheet-specific copy stays on its sheet. The page links to /stores/ for it
 * instead, which is where it is already argued properly.
 *
 * EVERY SENTENCE COMES FROM src/lib/capabilities.js AND NONE IS WRITTEN HERE.
 * That module's own header names the reason: §3 of the signed website Scope is
 * the source under all of it, and a second copy written in a sales voice drifts
 * the moment one of them is softened in a copy pass. A client then signs one
 * thing having read another.
 */
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import { SCOPED, HEADINGS, INTROS, assertCapabilities } from '../lib/capabilities.js';
/* DERIVED, 2026-09-13, AND IT WAS RIGHT AT THE TIME — which is the point. SCOPED
   held six and three sentences on this page typed "Six", so nothing was wrong
   yet. /websites/ had the identical shape and its list gained a seventh item on
   09-11; the sentence above its grid went on saying six until somebody read the
   page. This one is the same defect with the clock not yet run out.

   THE COUNT SURVIVED IN THE HEADER ABOVE UNTIL 2026-09-13, in two sentences
   that had been left alone because a comment is not copy. It is the worse half
   of the same defect: a stale figure lasts longest in a comment and is trusted
   most there, which is the argument /services/ already makes beside its own
   advisory panel. Both now name the list instead of its length. */
import { countWord } from '../lib/count-words.js';
const scopedWord = countWord(SCOPED.length, { capital: true });
import { ArrowUpRight, FileText } from '@lucide/astro';

assertCapabilities('quoted.astro');

const origin = new URL(Astro.site!).origin;

const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': `${origin}/quoted/#page`,
    name: 'Work I quote before it starts',
    description:
      `${scopedWord} things this practice builds that are priced on their own, in writing, before any of them begins — rather than folded into a build price.`,
    isPartOf: { '@id': `${origin}/#website` },
  },
];
---

<MarketingLayout
  title="Work I quote before it starts"
  description={`${scopedWord} things I build that carry their own price, agreed in writing before anything begins. Not extras, not refusals — separate work, named up front.`}
  schema={schema}
>
  {/* ── Hero ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      D113 CARRIED OFF THE HOME PAGE, 2026-09-13. This page ran two bands, both
      on canvas, at 6xl and 4xl — a width difference of two inches on a page
      whose second band is nearly all of the scroll. And that second band was a
      single column of identical `panel p-7` items, which is the complaint one
      level down from where D113 found it: /services/ ran five of those in one
      section and the answer there was a narrower column, not a new color.

      Three bands now. The column walks feature, popout, popout; the ground
      walks canvas, surface, canvas; and the closing coda is tightened under
      both. Every neighbor differs in at least two of ground, column and rhythm,
      and not one of them differs in the palette.

      THE NARROW COLUMN IS THE POINT OF THE MIDDLE BAND. A stack of items built
      the same way reads as one thing repeating no matter what the band around
      it does, and the cheapest thing that separates them is giving the stack a
      measure a reader's eye can hold — which is the reading in
      src/components/kit/industrial.js under WHY THREE AND NOT SEVEN, applied to
      a list rather than to a page.

      NOTHING MOVED AND NOTHING WAS CUT. The coda is the paragraph and the ask
      that already closed the second band, in the order they were written in;
      they are a band of their own now because a pointer at work that lives
      somewhere else is not another entry in the list above it. */}
  <Section width="feature" class="border-b border-line">
    <Fragment slot="backdrop">
      <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    </Fragment>
      <p class="mono-label">Real work · its own price · agreed before it starts</p>
      <h1 class="statement mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        {HEADINGS.scoped}.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        {INTROS.scoped}
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        None of this is an extra tacked onto an invoice later. Each one is named, priced and agreed
        before it begins, and you decide whether it happens at all. If you never ask for any of it,
        a build costs exactly what the build costs.
      </p>
  </Section>

  {/* ── The list itself, on the one ground this page paints ──────────────── */}
  <Section tone="surface" width="popout">
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-5">
      {
        SCOPED.map((item) => (
          <li class="panel flex flex-col gap-3 p-7">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <FileText class="mt-1 size-5 shrink-0 text-accent" />
              <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">{item.what}</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{item.detail}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </Section>

  {/* ── The coda: the one that lives elsewhere, and the ask ─────────────────
      Back to canvas and the shortest rhythm, against the banded list directly
      above. Same column, deliberately: this is still addressed to the reader
      who has just finished that list, and moving the measure under them would
      read as a new subject rather than as the end of this one. */}
  <Section width="popout" density="tight">
    {/* THE STORE CONFIGURATOR IS NAMED AND NOT REPEATED. See the header: it is
        stores-only copy in the capability module, and the argument for it lives
        on the page that can make it properly. A link is honest; a second copy
        of the sentence is the drift this whole module exists to prevent. */}
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      One more sits with the work it belongs to. If you sell something a customer has to configure
      before buying — a size, a finish, a cut to length — that builder is quoted work too, and the
      quote covers keeping it working when your platform changes.{' '}
      <a class="link-sweep" href="/stores/">It is argued on the selling-online comparison →</a>
    </p>

    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      {/* `btn` CARRIES THE SHAPE AND `btn-primary` ONLY THE PAINT, and this
          call to action shipped without the first of them. global.css:474 is
          where the padding, the inline-flex row and the corner radius live;
          :495 adds the gradient and the glow on top. Alone, the second paints a
          hard-edged rectangle with the label pressed against its sides and the
          arrow wrapped onto a second line — which is what the phone capture
          taken for this pass actually showed. Found 2026-09-13 by looking at
          the page, not by any gate: nothing here measures a control's geometry,
          and the color it paints was never the part that was wrong. */}
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact/?intent=quote">
        Ask what yours would cost <ArrowUpRight class="size-4" />
      </a>
      <a class="link-sweep text-sm" href="/services/">Or start with what a build includes →</a>
    </div>
  </Section>
</MarketingLayout>
